  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0030\" consists of five tables. The main table is \"auth_user\", which stores user information including their ID, password, last login time, whether they are a superuser, their username, first and last names, email, whether they are staff, whether they are active, and the date they joined. The \"auth_user\" table has a primary key of \"id\".\nThe other four tables are \"push_notifications_apnsdevice\", \"push_notifications_gcmdevice\", \"push_notifications_webpushdevice\", and \"push_notifications_wnsdevice\". These tables store device information for push notifications. They include fields like name, active status, date created, device ID, registration ID, user ID, and application ID. They also have a foreign key \"user_id\" that references the \"id\" in the \"auth_user\" table. This ensures referential integrity and links these tables to the \"auth_user\" table.\nThe \"push_notifications_apnsdevice\", \"push_notifications_gcmdevice\", \"push_notifications_webpushdevice\", and \"push_notifications_wnsdevice\" tables all have a primary key of \"id\". The \"push_notifications_apnsdevice\" table has a field for \"device_id\" which is a character field, \"push_notifications_gcmdevice\" has a field for \"device_id\" which is a big integer field, \"push_notifications_webpushdevice\" has fields for \"p256dh\" and \"auth\" which are both character fields, and \"push_notifications_wnsdevice\" has a field for \"device_id\" which is a character field. All these fields allow null values. The \"registration_id\" field in all these tables is a long text field and is not nullable. The \"active\" field in all these tables is a tiny integer field and is not nullable. The \"date_created\" field in all these tables is a datetime field and allows null values. The \"user_id\" field in all these tables is an integer field and allows null values. The \"application_id\" field in all these tables is a character field and allows null values.\nThis schema is designed to efficiently store and manage user and device information for push notifications. It ensures data integrity by using foreign keys to link the \"auth_user\" table to the device tables. It also ensures that all fields except \"registration_id\" are nullable, allowing for flexibility in data entry.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a mobile push notification system using Django. This system allows sending push notifications to mobile and web devices through multiple channels, including APNS (Apple Push Notification Service), FCM/GCM (Firebase Cloud Messaging/Google Cloud Messaging), WNS (Windows Notification Service), and WebPush. The system utilizes core Device models to manage and send notifications.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to enable efficient delivery of push notifications to a variety of devices. The system should facilitate sending both single and bulk notifications, handle device registrations, and update existing devices based on their registration IDs.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- End Users: Individuals receiving notifications on their devices.\n- Administrators: Manage devices and notifications via the admin panel.\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the system within Django applications.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Device Models: Implement models for different notification services (GCMDevice, APNSDevice, WNSDevice, WebPushDevice) with key attributes like name, active status, user linkage, device ID, and registration ID.\n- Notification Sending: Support sending notifications through various services either to individual devices or in bulk.\n- Admin Panel: Provide an interface to manage devices and facilitate testing of notifications, including a feature to handle duplicate registration IDs by updating existing records.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: System must efficiently manage and send notifications to a potentially large number of devices.\n- Maintainability: Clean and adaptable code structure to allow easy updates and maintenance.\n- Performance: Ensure quick delivery of notifications with minimal delay for end users.\n- Security: Secure handling of user data and notifications, including proper management of user authentication and registration IDs.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0030.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0030\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_superuser` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `username` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(30) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(150) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(254) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_joined` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_apnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `device_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_id` longtext NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_apnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_gcmdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `device_id` bigint(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_id` longtext NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_gcmdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_webpushdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `p256dh` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `auth` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_id` longtext NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_webpushdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_wnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `date_created` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `device_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_id` longtext NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `application_id` char(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_wnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
